 Part 2: Critical Quote Analysis

Quote 1 (Page 8)

“After all, it is a question of artistic feeling, and it is a great mistake to suppose that without this you can produce artistic work.”

I think John Andrews means that creating art is not just about knowing how to use a camera or other tools. It is also about having creativity, imagination, and the ability to see beauty in things.
A person can know everything about photography but still need artistic feeling to make a meaningful picture.

To me, artistic feeling means taking time to think about what you want to create. Instead of just taking a picture, an artist pays attention to the lighting, background, and mood. Andrews explains that artists should be patient and wait for the right moment. I learned that good art comes from both skill and creativity.



 Quote 2 (Page 12)

“Emerson has well expressed it when he defined art as ‘Nature passed through the alembic of man.’”

I understand this quote to mean that art is more than just copying what we see in nature. An artist uses imagination, emotions, and personal ideas to turn something ordinary into something special. The word “alembic” means something that changes or transforms materials, so in this quote, it represents the human mind.

For example, two people can look at the same beautiful landscape but create very different artworks. This happens because everyone has their own feelings and ideas. I think Emerson and Andrews are saying that art becomes meaningful when we add our own creativity and personal touch to what we see.



Quote 3 (Page 12)

“Photography is a most useful and curious invention and of the greatest value for the record of plain facts about persons and places, but it is not a fine art at all and can never be made one.”

In this quote, Philip Gilbert Hamerton believes that photography is useful for capturing real people, places, and events, but he does not consider it fine art. He thinks a camera mainly records what is already there instead of creating something through human imagination.

In his view, fine art should involve personal creativity, emotion, and the ability to change or interpret what we see. However, I think photography can also involve creativity because photographers choose the lighting, angle, background, and subject.

This connects to Emerson's idea because a photographer can use personal imagination and artistic choices to create something meaningful. Even though the camera takes the picture, the person behind it still makes important decisions. Andrews also recognizes that photography can have artistic value when guided by someone with artistic feeling.



Part 3: The Family of the Arts and the Contemporary AI Controversy

 The Kinship of Media

I think traditional painting, photography, and digital art are connected because they let people express their creativity in different ways. Painting uses brushes and colours, photography uses a camera, and digital art uses technology. Even though the tools are different, they can all create beautiful and meaningful artwork.

I believe AI-generated art can also be part of the art family because people can use their ideas and imagination to guide the results. John Andrews believed artistic feeling matters, and Emerson believed art involves human imagination. I think these ideas can also apply to AI art when a person makes thoughtful creative choices.

However, I understand Hamerton's point of view because AI does much of the work of creating the actual image. This makes AI art different from traditional painting and drawing.



Evaluating the Colorado State Fair Verdict

I think it was fair for Jason M. Allen to win the Colorado State Fair digital art competition because he used AI as a tool to create his artwork, *Théâtre D’opéra Spatial*. He explained that he used Midjourney, and his work involved writing prompts, choosing images, and improving the final result.

I understand why some artists were upset because they spend a lot of time drawing and painting by hand. They may feel that using AI makes creating artwork easier. Hamerton would probably agree with them because he believed that machines cannot replace human artistic creativity.

However, I also think Andrews and Emerson might see AI differently.
They believed human imagination and artistic choices matter. Even when a machine is involved, a person can still decide the colours, mood, composition, and meaning of the artwork.





My Personal Opinion and Conclusion

In my opinion, AI-generated images can be considered art when people use their creativity and imagination to make them. 
I do not think AI should completely replace traditional artists, but I believe it can be another way for people to express their ideas.

Creating my own AI image for this assignment helped me understand this better. 
I had to think about what kind of image I wanted and choose the one that I liked the most. I learned that technology can help us create things we might not be able to draw ourselves.

Overall, this assignment taught me that art is not only about the tools we use. 
It is also about our ideas, feelings, imagination, and the meaning behind what we create. I believe both traditional art and AI-generated art can be appreciated in different ways.
